    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d249">

                <pb facs="0515" n="381" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg381"/>

                <head>No. 249.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Chang Yen
                    Hoon</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-01-10"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 10,
                        1888</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of yesterday’s date, giving me knowledge of the communications to
                    you from your Government in relation to the coming hither of an envoy from the
                    Government of Corea to the United States.</p>
                <p>Shortly after having been thus advised by your note I received from the envoy of
                    Corea, Mr. Pak Chung Yang, a letter by the hands of his secretary asking an
                    interview with me at this Department for the purpose of making an arrangement
                    for the presentation of his credential letter to the President.</p>
                <p>To this I have replied that I shall expect to receive the Corean minister on
                    Friday next, the 13th instant, at 12 o’clock, noon, and shall thereafter at an
                    early day accompany him to be presented to the President in official audience,
                    in the usual manner.</p>
                <p>Accept sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
